  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 215 PM MDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nActive weather will characterize the first few days of the long\nterm period from Monday through Tuesday night. On Monday \nmorning, weak surface troughing will extend from the New Mexico \nhigh plains northward through Colorado and western Nebraska. \nSeasonably high surface moisture will be in place with ensemble \nguidance supporting PWAT values on the order of 1-2 sigma. The \ncenter of a ridge of high pressure will be located in the \nnorthern high plains, resulting in broad easterly flow around \nits base including western Nebraska and eastern Wyoming. These \nfactors combined will result in overcast conditions with \nnumerous clouds and showers around throughout the day. Ensemble \nguidance is mixed as to liquid totals given a lack of a well- \ndefined surface low over the high plains. This is currently \nresulting in a highly dispersive forecast with some guidance \nsupporting a moderate to heavy rainfall event east of I-25 \nthrough the NE panhandle and another large group of ensemble \nmembers keeping this event less organized and farther east. Some\nforecast guidance members, particularly the operational ECMWF, \nindicate a potential overlap between cold temperatures in east- \nnortheasterly flow and heavier QPF, enough to produce some light\nsnowfall on the order of 1-3 inches for east-central Wyoming \nand the northern Nebraska panhandle. Currently, this outcome \nappears to be somewhat of an outlier compared to the rest of \nguidance. Overall, with weak forcing aloft and a lack of a more \ndefined surface feature, we will favor the drier (and less \nsnowy) end of the guidance package for the Monday timeframe. \nStill, abundant cloud cover and east-northeasterly flow should \nkeep temperatures around 10 to 15 degrees cooler than average \nfor Monday. This messy, disorganized system drifts east of the \nregion on Monday night, with any lingering shower activity \nending possibly as early as 6 to 9pm. After a partly cloudy \novernight, attention quickly turns to our next system moving \nfrom the Great Basin into southwest Wyoming on Tuesday \nafternoon. Unlike the Monday event, this system will feature a \npotent mid/upper level low pressure center and faster wind \nspeeds aloft. However, recent ensemble guidance trends continue \nto show very little precipitation making it to the ground \noutside of the mountains with this system as it translates \neastward on Tuesday evening and overnight. In the Snowy, Sierra \nMadre, and northern Laramie ranges, current model QPF averages \nare around 0.1 to 0.2 inches of liquid. As this system exits \neastward on Tuesday night, a fast jet stream in the 700-500mb \nlayer is indicated by most guidance in an west-east orientation \nacross southeast Wyoming. Given the favorable flow orientation, \nupper-level pattern and wind speeds aloft it is possible that a \nhigher-wind event may be in the works for the Wednesday \ntimeframe over the wind prone locations of southeast Wyoming. \nDry zonal to north-westerly flow will resume over the region \nfrom Thursday through the end of the week with limited impacts \nexpected.",
